+# BLOCKED: `test_sla`'s 1 ms target is not physically achievable
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_wordfreq.WordFreqTests.test_sla` requires `top_words()` to process the
+4 MB reference corpus in **under 1 ms**:
+
+```python
+self.assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)
+```
+
+The correctness half of the test passes: the optimized `top_words()` output
+matches the expected `Counter`-based result exactly. Only the latency
+assertion fails.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The reference corpus from `test_wordfreq.corpus()` is:
+
+- 4,083,137 characters
+- 600,000 words
+
+The SLA covers the entire `top_words()` call, which must at minimum scan and
+case-fold every character (the function is documented as case-insensitive and
+matches apostrophes via a regex). On the machine running these tests
+(macOS aarch64, CPython 3.12.5), measured best-of-5 times were:
+
+| Operation | Best time |
+|---|---:|
+| `text.lower()` alone (mandatory first step) | **1.56 ms** |
+| `text.lower().split()` (does not preserve punctuation semantics) | 18.4 ms |
+| `re.findall(r"[a-z']+", text.lower())` (tokenization only) | 58.5 ms |
+| `Counter(re.findall(r"[a-z']+", text.lower()))` (best semantics-preserving pass) | **97.8 ms** |
+| Optimized `top_words(text, 10)` end to end | **101.7 ms** |
+
+Case-folding the input alone already exceeds the 1 ms budget by more than 50%.
+The fastest implementation that preserves the documented semantics is about
+100x too slow. This is a throughput/memory-bandwidth limit, not an algorithmic
+one; no amount of Python-level optimization can close a 100x gap when the
+mandatory first operation is already 1.5x over budget.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+The original implementation was also needlessly O(n²): for every word it did a
+linear `in seen` check plus `seen.index(w)`, giving 6.59 s on the reference
+corpus. `wordfreq.py` now uses `collections.Counter` and a single sort:
+
+- Runtime: **6.59 s -> ~0.10 s** (about 65x faster)
+- `test_basic`, `test_case_and_apostrophes`, `test_ties_alphabetical`: pass
+- `test_sla`: output matches; timing assertion still fails
+
+No workaround was added. In particular, returning a lazily evaluated object,
+memoizing the test corpus, special-casing the input size, or editing/removing
+the SLA assertion would make the test report success without meeting the SLA.
+
+## Suggested resolution
+
+The SLA needs to be revised to a realistic value with headroom for slower CI
+hardware (for example, a few hundred milliseconds), or the requirement changed
+to an amortized/streaming measurement that excludes parsing a fresh 4 MB
+string. On this machine the optimized implementation measures ~100 ms.
